Recall that the symmetric difference of two sets \( A \) and \( B \) is the set \( A \triangle B = (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) = (A \cup B) \setminus (A \cap B) \). Prove that if \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), then \( B \subseteq A \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To prove that \( B \subseteq A \) under the condition \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), we proceed as follows:

1. **Assume \( B \notin A \)**: Suppose for contradiction that \( B \notin A \). This means there exists an element \( x \in B \) such that \( x \notin A \).

2. **Since \( x \in B \) and \( x \notin A \), \( x \in B \setminus A \)**: By definition of \( B \setminus A \), if \( x \in B \) and \( x \notin A \), then \( x \in B \setminus A \).

3. **Since \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), \( B \setminus A \subseteq A \)**: By definition of symmetric difference, \( A \triangle B = (B \setminus A) \cup (A \setminus B) \). Since \( A \triangleright B \subseteq A \), it follows that \( (B \setminus A) \cup (A \setminus B) \subseteq A \). Since \( A \setminus B \subseteq A \), it follows that \( B \setminus A \subseteq A \).

