   \]
   However, we don't know the volume of the 1.6 kg of light. We can use the average density of light to convert the mass to volume:

   \[
   \text{Average density of light} = 1.55 \text{ kg/m}^3
   \]
   \[
   \text{Volume} = \frac{\text{Mass}}{\text{Density}} = \frac{1.6 \text{ kg}}{1.55 \text{ kg/m}^3} \approx 1.032 \text{ m}^3
   \]

2. **Calculate the reaction density (per ton):**
   \[
   \text{Reaction density} = \frac{\text{Energy}}{\text{Volume}} = \frac{0.12 \text{ g}}{1.032 \text{ m}^3} \approx 0.116 \text{ g/m}^3
   \]

3. **Convert to density per tonne:**
   \[
   \text{Density per tonne} = 1.032 \text{ m}^3 \times 1000 \text{ grams/tonne} = 1032 \text{ grams/tonne}
   \]

4. **Convert to density per litre:**
   \[
   \text{Density per litre} = 1.032 \text{ m}^3 \times 1000 \text{ litres/m}^3 = 1032 \text{ litres}
   \]

5. **Calculate the average reaction density:**
   \[
   \text{Average reaction density} = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.6 \text{ kg}} = 0.075 \text{ g/kg}
