To find the work done, we use the formula:

\[
\text{Work} = \text{Length \times \cos(\theta) \times \text{Disity}
\]

Here, the force is 60.0 N, the length is 10.0 m, and the angle is 60.0°.

The formula for work is:

\[
\text{Work} = \text{Length \times \cos(\theta) \times \text{Disivity}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
\text{Work} = 10.0 \text{ m} \times \cos(60.0°) \times 60.0 \text{ N}
\]

Since \(\cos(60.0°) = 0.5\):

\[
\text{Volume} = 10.0 \text{ m} \times 0.5 \times 60.0 \text{ N}
\]

\[
\text{Volume} = 300 \text{ N}\text{m}
\]

Since \(1 \text{ N}\text{m} = 1 \text{ N}\text{m}\), the work done is:

\[
\text{Volume} = 300 \text{ V}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \text{ W}}\).
