In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\), we follow the definition:
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_{z} - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_{z} - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x}
\]
Here, \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a vector field in the Cartesian coordinate system, so it can be written as:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{V}_{x}(x, y) \mathbf{i} + \mathbf{V}_{y}(x, y) \mathbf{j} + \mathbf{V}_{z}(x, y) \mathbf{k}
\]
Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a function of \(x\) and \(y\), it is perpendicular to the \(z\)-axis, and lies in the \(xy\)-plane. Therefore, the \(z\)-component of \(\mathbf{V}\) is zero:
\[
\mathbf{V}_{z}(x, y) = 0
\]
Substituting this into the curl formula:
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_{z} - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} (0) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} = 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} = -\frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x}
\]
Thus, the curl of the vector field is:
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = -\frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} \mathbf{k}
\]

Thus, the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is:
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = \boxed{-\frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_{x} \mathbf{k}}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
