","The decay length (\(L_{\text{decay}}\)) of an exponentially decayating exponential wave is related to the difference between the effective frequency (\(n_{\text{eff}}\)) and the feed frequency (\(n_f\)) by the formula:

\[
L_{\text{decay}} = \frac{1}{2\pi \left(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f\right)}
\]

Here, \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective frequency of the wave in the cavity, and \(n_f\) is the frequency of the field in the feed (or-free) field.

Given that \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\) and the wavelength (\(L\)) is 1 μm (1000 m), we can calculate the decay length:

\[
L_{\text{drawn}} = \frac{1}{2\pi \times 0.3} \approx \frac{1}{0.585 \pi} \approx 0.54 \text{ m} \approx 0.54 \text{ m}
\]

