<USER_REQUEST>
A particle of mass 30 kg is subjected to a force of 32 N. Calculate the acceleration of the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Using Newton's second law of motion:
\[ F = ma \]
where:
- \( F = 32 \, \text{N} \) (force applied)
- \( m = 30 \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the particle)

Rearranging to solve for acceleration \( a \):
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{32}{30} \]

Simplifying the fraction:
\[ a = \frac{16}{15} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]

Thus, the acceleration of the particle is \(\boxed{\dfrac{16}{15} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the acceleration, we can use the formula:

\[
\text{Acceleration} = \frac{\text{Force}}{\text{Mass}}
\]

Given:
\[
\text{Mass} = 30 \text{ kg}
\]
\[
\text{Force} = 32 \text{ N}
\]

Substituting the values into the formula:

\[
\text{Acceleration} = \frac{32 \text{ N}}{30 \text{ kg}} = 1.06666666666 \text{ m/s}^2
\]

Rounding to two decimal places, the acceleration is \(\boxed{1.07 \text{ m/s}^2}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

